conomy because  it's not because when you look at what  it means this is why I'm saying like  this is a lie that's been purported  by Wall Street when a company fails it  does not fire their employees it goes  through a package bankruptcy right if  anything what happens is the people who  have the pensions inside those companies  the employees of these companies end up  owning more of the company the people  that get wiped out are the speculators  that own the unsecured tranches of debt  for the folks that owned the equity and  by the way those are the rules of the  game that's right because these are the  people that purport to be the most  sophisticated investors in the world  they deserve to get wiped out but the  employees don't get wiped out so this is  a really important point just to start  with very often the justification for  bailing out a company is you've got to  save the employees now there are good  and bad reasons to bail out a company  but Chamath points out for these huge  companies like Airlines they don't just  go bankrupt lay everybody off say  bye-bye sell their airplanes for peanuts  and you never hear from them again  bankruptcy for these huge companies is a  process that includes restructuring it  often includes golden parachutes or big  payouts for the executives but it  includes all sorts of mechanisms for  reorganizing and very often not always  these bankruptcies work out okay for the  employees not all of them in every case  but in many cases and Shema this'  explaining that so now Scott Wapner  starts to wonder where Chamath is going  with this and it gets even better take a  look why does anybody do that understand  why does anybody deserve using your word  to get wiped out from a crisis created  like like this how does anybody is there  to get wiped out well good but just be  clear like who are we talking about  we're talking about a hedge fund that  serves a bunch of billionaire family  offices who cares let them get wiped out  who cares they don't get the summer in  the Hamptons who cares I mean they're  not you don't think the employees of  these companies owned stocks  I mean owned their stocks owned the  company stocks you can you can look on  Bloomberg and you can see what  percentage of these companies are  typically owned by these be things are  owned by black rocks these things  they're owned by these huge you know  Amorphis organizations ultimately  downstream and the employee owns a few  hundred dollars or a few  thousand dollars of wives donors this is  a key moment the employees don't deserve  to get wiped out they're not engaging in  speculative activity of little benefit  to the economy they are just working  their jobs the billionaires and the  hedge funds and the speculators they  also don't deserve to get what wiped out  but this is risk reward the billionaires  and the hedge funds and the speculators  they are choosing to take a risk for a  possibly huge reward  you can't just bail them out of that  risk this is the risk they decided to  take on the risk and reward need to be  in concert they are risking getting  wiped out to possibly get the huge  reward of making crazy money they then  get to another ruse which is this idea  of hey but the employees are  shareholders if the share price collapse  that hurts the employees I've said this  before the stock market is not an  indicator of the health of the economy  it is true many companies have employees  stock purchase programs employees own  stock in their company to some degree  but as trim off  out it is disproportionately tiny  amounts of stock the stock is mostly  held by executives by investment firms  like Blackrock and others outside of the  company it is another ruse that is used  to justify bailing out every company  without actually thinking critically  about whether they should be bailed out  let's now check out just the last  portion like a natural disaster why does  anybody deserve to get wiped out  wouldn't that be immoral in and of  itself no because what's happening right  now is what I'll tell you is on Main  Street today people are getting wiped  out and right now rich CEOs are not  boards that had horrible governance are  not hedge funds are not people are six  million people just this week alone  basically saying holy mackerel I don't  know how I'm gonna make you know my own  expenses for the next few weeks days  months so it's happening today to  individual Americans and what we've done  is disproportionately prop up and  protect you know poor performing CEOs  companies and boards and you have to  watch these people out let me introduce  an idea here it's sort of a combination  of an evolutionary idea and an economic  idea the coronavirus is terrible it's a  pandemic it's a crisis there's endless  suffering death and destruction as a  result in a way it can also serve as a  sort of filter for example there's the  idea that our behavior on earth is so  damaging to the planet and then you look  at the pandemic air travels down 95%  global carbon emissions are down air is  clearing water is clearing pollution is  declining it's going above and beyond  our normal actions and something is  saying nope we're gonna reverse the  trend briefly and work as a sort of  filter when it comes to economics there  is a ton of non-productive speculative  vulture activity that is not  economically productive we talk about an  economic multiplier that's the factor by  which one type of economic activity  multiplies through the economy so you  build a factory or you finance some  mortgages these actions have different  multipliers a ton of the stuff that's  being propped up with these bailouts is  very low multiplier action speculation  derivative trading leverage stuff it  generates profit for the people doing it  but it is a very little economic value  maybe this crisis is an opportunity to  filter out some of that nonsense that  helps hedge funds make a ton of money  but is not particularly good for the  economy really interesting stuff check  out other interviews with trimethyl  Padilla that he's done recently very  interesting billionaire we'll take a  quick break and we have so much more  coming up there's a YouTube channel  we're partnering with that I would love  for you to check out it's a channel  called up flip you can get there by  clicking the link below make sure you  subscribe when you're there every week  up flip uploads a new video where you  get an exclusive behind-the-scenes look  at a different small business everything  from real estate firms coffee shops auto  repair shops each business owner shares  a ton of really interesting insight  about how they built their business what  it's like day-to-day how they got to  where they are fascinating to learn  about other people's business strategies  how they found success it's a great  channel for anyone thinking of starting  a business already  running one or just people interested in  how different business work perfect for  people who like TV shows like Shark Tank  and that type of thing check it out at  david pakman.com slash up flip the link  is underneath the video they are friends  of the david pakman show make sure to  subscribe when you're there click that  Bell icon to be notified when they  upload new videos
 

